.TH UNIFDEF 1.SH NAMEunifdef \- remove #ifdef sections for chosen symbols.SH SYNOPSIS\fBunifdef\fR[\-clt] [\-d\fIsym\fR] [\-u\fIsym\fR] [\-id\fIsym\fR] [\-iu\fIsym\fR] [file].SH DESCRIPTION\fBunifdef\fRcopies C source to the standard output with the\fB#ifdef\fR,\fR\fB#ifndef\fRand\fB#else\fRsections for the named symbols resolved: the branch that would becompiled is kept, the other dropped, and the directives themselves go.Everything else is left as it was.  At least one symbol must be named..SH OPTIONS.TP 12\fB\-d\fIsym\fR\fIsym\fRis defined.  Lower case, and no equals sign:\fB\-dOSK\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-u\fIsym\fR\fIsym\fRis undefined..TP 12\fB\-id\fIsym\fB\fR, \fI\-iu\fBsym\fRleave the lines inside\fIsym's\fRsections alone, as defined or undefined..TP 12\fB\-l\fRblank out removed lines instead of deleting them, so line numbers staythe same..TP 12\fB\-c\fRthe complement: keep the branches that would not be compiled..TP 12\fB\-t\fRplain text: ignore comments and quotes..SH EXIT STATUS0 when the output is the input unchanged, 1 when it differs, 2 ontrouble..SH EXAMPLES.nfunifdef -dOSK prog.c > prog.osk.c.fi.SH SEE ALSOcpp, ifdef